{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the Count of Tripoli from 1146 to 1177 and a leader of the Crusades He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Armenia Raymond III played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a close ally of King Amalric I\n\n* **Early Life** He was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at the age of six with his mother Helena acting as regent until he came of age Raymond III took power in 1154 and quickly established himself as a skilled military leader\n\nRaymond III was involved in several key battles including the Battle of Inab in 1149 where he fought alongside King Raymond IV of Lebanon and the Battle of Montgisard in 1177 where he led a small army to victory against the Ayyubid dynasty He also played a crucial role in the Siege of Homs in 1122 and the Siege of Damascus in 1148\n\n* **Marriage and Alliances** Raymond III married Agnes of Courtenay who was the daughter of King Conrad III of Germany and the niece of King Manuel I of Constantinople This marriage formed a strong alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Byzantine Empire Raymond III also formed alliances with the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller\n\nRaymond III was known for his bravery and military tactics however his death in 1177 led to a period of instability in the County of Tripoli He died without an heir and the county was passed to his nephew Hugh III Count of Cyprus \n\n* **Legacy** Raymond III is remembered as one of the most important figures of the Crusades and a skilled military leader His alliances with the Byzantine Empire and the military orders helped to secure the County of Tripoli for many years after his death Raymond III legacy continues to be studied by historians today due to his significant impact on the history of the Crusades", "logprobs": [{"